-- title:
--   The invariant derivation $D_w$ maps every per-coordinate box of $K[X, Y, Z]$ into itself and the torus ideal $(Y_iZ_i - 1)$ into itself
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field. Let $A_0, A_1$ be finite types and $R = K[X, Y, Z]$ the polynomial ring `MvPolynomial (A₀ ⊕ (A₁ × Bool)) K` in the variables $X_h$ ($h \in A_0$), $Y_i$ and $Z_i$ ($i \in A_1$), and $J_0 = (Y_iZ_i - 1)_{i \in A_1}$ its torus ideal (`torusIdeal`). For $w = (\xi, \eta) \in K^{A_0} \times K^{A_1}$, $D_w$ (`torusDeriv w`) is the invariant derivation $\sum_h \xi_h\,\partial/\partial X_h + \sum_i \eta_i (Y_i\,\partial/\partial Y_i - Z_i\,\partial/\partial Z_i)$ of $R$ (DALAG Lemma 8.11), and $D_l = D_{w_1} \circ \cdots \circ D_{w_t}$ (`torusDerivList l`) for a list $l = [w_1, \dots, w_t]$. Let $w \in K^{A_0} \times K^{A_1}$. Then $D_w$ maps every box `torusBox K A₀ A₁ T` (the span of the monomials of degree at most $T_h$ in $X_h$ and at most $T_i$ in the pair $Y_i, Z_i$) into itself, and maps $J_0$ into itself. The names `TorusGroup`, `torusPt`, `torusDeriv`, `torusDerivList`, `torusTransl`, `torusVanishing`, `torusZero`, `torusStab`, `standardSet`, `sectionIdeal`, `vanishOrd`, `hmePoint` and `obstructionSpan` come from the Definition `DiazMultiplicity` (module `Definitions.Def_DiazMultiplicity`), which builds on the published Definition `DiazHilbertBox` (`torusIdeal`, `torusBox`, `boxHilb`, `boxD`, `torusCoeffPoly`, `torusReduced`, `torusPoint`).
--
--   **Proof.** $D_w$ sends a monomial to a combination of the monomial itself (from $Y_i$, $Z_i$, which are eigenvectors) and of monomials with one $X_h$ fewer, so boxes are stable. $D_w(Y_iZ_i - 1) = 0$, and the Leibniz rule keeps the ideal generated by these elements stable.
--
--   **Novelty.** None claimed: a property of the invariant derivations of Lemma 8.11 of DALAG, Chapter 8 (by D. Roy), written at finite level and in coordinates; routine formalisation.
-- source:
--   M. Waldschmidt, Diophantine Approximation on Linear Algebraic Groups, Grundlehren der mathematischen Wissenschaften 326, Springer, 2000, Chapter 8 (by D. Roy), Lemma 8.11 (the invariant derivations of $\mathbb{G}_a^{d_0} \times \mathbb{G}_m^{d_1}$). C1.3 of the Diaz modulus mission. Formal proof: Diaz modulus mission, 30 October 2026 (C. Perassi).

import Definitions.Def_DiazMultiplicity

namespace DiazModulus

theorem torusDeriv_mem_torusBox {K A₀ A₁ : Type*} [Field K] (w : (A₀ → K) × (A₁ → K)) :
    (∀ (T : A₀ ⊕ A₁ → ℕ) (f : MvPolynomial (A₀ ⊕ (A₁ × Bool)) K), f ∈ torusBox K A₀ A₁ T → torusDeriv w f ∈ torusBox K A₀ A₁ T) ∧
      ∀ f ∈ torusIdeal K A₀ A₁, torusDeriv w f ∈ torusIdeal K A₀ A₁ := by
  sorry

end DiazModulus
